    {
        "name": "Ethylene Glycol Poisoning (Antifreeze)",
        "name_ja": "エチレングリコール中毒（不凍液）",
        "symptoms": {"vomiting", "excessive_thirst", "seizures", "lethargy"},
        "description": "Lethal antifreeze poisoning causing rapid kidney failure. Antidote (fomepizole or ethanol) MUST be given within 8-12 hours of ingestion before renal metabolites accumulate. Lethal dose in dogs: 4.4 mL/kg. 3 clinical phases: Phase I (30 min-12h: inebriation, polyuria); Phase II (12-24h: cardiopulmonary signs); Phase III (24-72h: oliguric renal failure — often fatal at this stage).",
        "description_ja": "致死的な不凍液中毒。腎障害を引き起こす代謝産物が蓄積する前に摂取後8〜12時間以内に解毒剤（フォメピゾールまたはエタノール）を投与しなければならない。犬の致死量：4.4 mL/kg。3段階の臨床経過：I期（30分〜12h：酩酊・多尿）；II期（12〜24h：心肺症状）；III期（24〜72h：乏尿性腎不全—この段階では多くの場合致死的）。",
        "pathophysiology": "Ethylene glycol ingested → metabolized by hepatic alcohol dehydrogenase (ADH) → glycolaldehyde → glycolate → oxalate. (1) High-anion-gap metabolic acidosis from glycolate accumulation; (2) Calcium oxalate crystal deposition in renal tubules → acute tubular necrosis → oliguric renal failure. Critical window: ethylene glycol itself (before metabolism) is less toxic — antidote prevents ADH metabolism.",
        "pathophysiology_ja": "エチレングリコール摂取→肝臓のADHにより代謝→グリコアルデヒド→グリコール酸→シュウ酸。(1)グリコール酸蓄積による高アニオンギャップ代謝性アシドーシス；(2)シュウ酸カルシウム結晶の腎尿細管沈着→急性尿細管壊死→乏尿性腎不全。重要：エチレングリコール自体（代謝前）は比較的低毒性—解毒剤はADHによる代謝を阻止することが目的。",
        "causes": "Automotive antifreeze (ethylene glycol) ingestion. Sweet taste attracts dogs. Garage antifreeze leaks, puddle contamination. Winter season. Even small amounts can be lethal. Treatment window closes at 8-12 hours from ingestion.",
        "causes_ja": "自動車用不凍液（エチレングリコール）の摂取。甘味があるため犬が好んで飲む。ガレージの不凍液漏れ、水たまりの汚染水。冬季に多い。少量でも致死的。治療の窓は摂取後8〜12時間で閉じる。",
        "recommended_tests": [
            "Ethylene glycol rapid test kit (Kacey Clinical Systems): positive within 30-60 minutes of ingestion; false positives at >8-12h due to metabolism; confirms ingestion before renal phase",
            "Urinalysis (sediment with polarizing light microscopy): calcium oxalate monohydrate crystals (needle/envelope-shaped) in urine — pathognomonic for Phase II-III; dilute urine initially then concentrated/oliguric",
            "Serum chemistry: BUN/creatinine (elevated Phase III), electrolytes (Na, K, Ca — hypocalcemia from oxalate chelation), osmolality, anion gap (elevated early = glycolate)",
            "Arterial or venous blood gas: high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis (glycolate) — severity indicates urgency; pH <7.2 = severe",
            "CBC: stress leukocytosis; hematocrit for fluid status",
            "Serum ethylene glycol concentration (reference lab): quantitative but slow — less useful for immediate management",
            "Abdominal ultrasound: cortical hyperechogenicity (calcium oxalate deposition in renal cortex) — specific sign of Phase III; acute renal failure assessment",
        ],
        "treatment": "EMERGENCY — antidote within 8-12 hours. Phase I-II (within 8-12h of ingestion): (1) Fomepizole (4-methylpyrazole, 4-MP) — dog antidote of choice: 20 mg/kg IV loading dose, then 15 mg/kg IV at 12h, 15 mg/kg at 24h, 5 mg/kg at 36h; much safer than ethanol. (2) Ethanol (if fomepizole unavailable): 20% ethanol solution 5 mL/kg IV over 15 min, then 1.3 mL/kg/h CRI for 48 hours (monitor CNS depression, hypoglycemia, hypothermia). GI decontamination ONLY if within 30-60 minutes: emesis (apomorphine 0.03 mg/kg IV or 0.04 mg/kg IM) + activated charcoal 1-3 g/kg PO (limited efficacy for alcohols). IV fluid diuresis (0.9% NaCl or LRS 3-5 mL/kg/h) to maintain renal perfusion and diuresis — correct acidosis with sodium bicarbonate if pH <7.1. Phase III (renal failure): hemodialysis or peritoneal dialysis if available — removes EG and metabolites; intensive supportive care (fluid therapy, anti-emetics, anti-ulcer treatment); prognosis poor without dialysis. Monitor urine output q1-2h; catheterize for oliguric/anuric dogs. Thiamine 100 mg/dog IM BID (redirects glycolate metabolism away from oxalate pathway).",
        "treatment_ja": "緊急—8〜12時間以内に解毒剤投与。I〜II期（摂取後8〜12時間以内）：(1)フォメピゾール（4-メチルピラゾール、4-MP）—犬の推奨解毒剤：20 mg/kg IV負荷、その後15 mg/kg IV（12h）、15 mg/kg（24h）、5 mg/kg（36h）；エタノールより安全。(2)エタノール（フォメピゾール入手不能時）：20%エタノール液5 mL/kg IV（15分で投与）、その後1.3 mL/kg/h CRI（48時間；CNS抑制・低血糖・低体温のモニタリング）。消化管除染（摂取後30〜60分以内のみ）：催吐（アポモルヒネ0.03 mg/kg IV）＋活性炭1〜3 g/kg PO（アルコールへの有効性は限定的）。IV輸液による利尿（0.9% NaClまたはLRS 3〜5 mL/kg/h）—腎血流と尿量を維持；pH<7.1の場合は炭酸水素ナトリウムで酸血症補正。III期（腎不全）：透析（血液透析または腹膜透析）が利用可能であれば実施—EGと代謝産物を除去；集中的支持療法（輸液・制吐薬・消化器保護）；透析なしでは予後不良。尿量を1〜2時間毎にモニタリング；乏尿/無尿犬には尿道カテーテル留置。チアミン100 mg/犬 IM BID（グリコール酸の代謝をシュウ酸経路から転換）。",
        "prognosis": "Phase I-II (treated within 8-12 hours): 70-90% survival with fomepizole. Phase III (renal failure established): poor without dialysis (<20% survival); guarded with dialysis (30-60%). Time to treatment is the most critical prognostic factor. Dogs presenting oliguric/anuric at Phase III have very poor prognosis without dialysis. Renal recovery rare once established AKI from oxalate deposition.",
        "prognosis_ja": "I〜II期（8〜12時間以内に治療）：フォメピゾールで70〜90%の生存率。III期（腎不全確立後）：透析なしでは不良（<20%の生存率）；透析ありでは慎重（30〜60%）。治療開始までの時間が最も重要な予後因子。III期で乏尿/無尿の状態で来院した犬は透析なしでは予後非常に不良。シュウ酸沈着による確立したAKIからの腎回復は稀。",
        "prevention": "Complete isolation of antifreeze from pet access. Immediate cleanup of leaks. Use propylene glycol-based alternatives (safer). Restrict pet access to garages. Owner education on sweet taste appeal to dogs.",
        "prevention_ja": "不凍液のペットからの完全隔離、漏れの即時清掃、プロピレングリコール系の安全な代替品の使用（より安全）、ガレージへのペットアクセス制限。甘味による犬の誘引についてオーナー教育。",
        "urgency": "emergency",
    },
